# Open-source / ecosystem-distributed tool

Marketing an OSS tool — or anything distributed through an ecosystem (package registry, extension marketplace, plugin) — sells trust in the ecosystem position, not just the artifact.

Do:
- Name the adoption mode and market to it: using in-house, integrating into a product, contributing, commercializing. Each has different evaluators and risks; "adoption" generically targets none. (⚠ abstract-level evidence)
- Lead enterprise-facing OSS messaging with security posture, open-standards compliance, and support availability — the three measured drivers of vendor trust, which in turn drives adoption intent.
- Show ecosystem health as evidence: release cadence, maintainer count, issue responsiveness, downstream dependents. Governance transparency (license clarity, roadmap process) is a trust claim enterprises can verify themselves. When drafting, include at least one concrete ecosystem-health or governance line in the asset — ask for the real numbers or mark [placeholders]; a pitch that could equally describe a closed-source product has not marketed the ecosystem position.
- Address supply-chain exposure explicitly: provenance, signing, dependency policy. Readers evaluate the whole chain, not just your code.

Don't:
- Stars/download counts as the trust argument — reference-group-specific proof still applies.
- "Trust scores" or badges as proof points without saying what they measure — trust-evaluation approaches have known coverage and objectivity gaps.
- Treat the community as a marketing channel only; for the integrating and contributing modes, contribution paths are part of the product surface.
